WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:03.336
-Our first guest tonight is
a talented actress you know from

00:00:03.336 --> 00:00:05.004
films such as "21 Bridges,"

00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:06.706
"American Woman"
and "Factory Girl."

00:00:06.706 --> 00:00:08.641
She stars in
"Anatomy of a Scandal,"

00:00:08.641 --> 00:00:10.810
which begins streaming
April 15th on Netflix.

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:12.211
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.211 --> 00:00:14.380
-You're not going to be
springing any surprises on us,

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.749
are you, Sophie?
-Such as?

00:00:16.749 --> 00:00:18.684
-Well, announcing
that you're leaving,

00:00:18.684 --> 00:00:20.286
putting on your side
of the story.

00:00:20.286 --> 00:00:22.388
-Do you need to ask?
-Of course I do.

00:00:22.388 --> 00:00:24.924
-Of course she's not leaving.

00:00:24.924 --> 00:00:26.359
I'm sorry, darling,
I didn't mean to.

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:28.160
-Of course I'm not leaving.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:31.230
We've been married for 12 years.
We share two children.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:34.467
Why would I throw all that away
over a stupid mistake?

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:36.235
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:36.235 --> 00:00:38.037
our friend Sienna Miller,
everyone.

00:00:38.037 --> 00:00:40.072
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:40.072 --> 00:00:47.079 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:47.079 --> 00:00:54.087 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:54.087 --> 00:00:55.521
It is so lovely to see you
in person again.

00:00:55.521 --> 00:00:58.258
-Thank you. So nice to see
everybody in person again.

00:00:58.258 --> 00:00:59.325
-I know, isn't it wonderful

00:00:59.325 --> 00:01:00.393
to be in front of people
and everything?

00:01:00.393 --> 00:01:01.827
-A live human
with a live audience.

00:01:01.827 --> 00:01:03.362
-A live audience.
It's very nice.

00:01:03.362 --> 00:01:05.431
I do want to follow up from
the last time you were here.

00:01:05.431 --> 00:01:09.535
You were talking about -- You
wanted to find the sort of work

00:01:09.535 --> 00:01:11.870
that maybe your daughter
could engage in.

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:14.007
Because you do a lot of sort of
R-rated adult fare.

00:01:14.007 --> 00:01:15.140
-This is not that.

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:16.041
-No, this is not a show
for kids.

00:01:16.041 --> 00:01:17.309
-Nope. No.

00:01:17.309 --> 00:01:19.678
Because you don't do,
you know, again,

00:01:19.678 --> 00:01:22.515
these are wonderful,
but like, you know, adult fare.

00:01:22.515 --> 00:01:25.151
Does your daughter even --
What does she think you do?

00:01:25.151 --> 00:01:26.753
I mean, she must know
you're an actor.

00:01:26.753 --> 00:01:28.021
-You know, she's going to be 10
in July,

00:01:28.021 --> 00:01:29.422
so she's seen
a little bit of this.

00:01:29.422 --> 00:01:31.657
And we fast-forwarded
the really, highly inappropriate

00:01:31.657 --> 00:01:33.326
moments of which
there are a few.

00:01:33.326 --> 00:01:36.762
But you know, she actually --
I was doing a play in London

00:01:36.762 --> 00:01:38.931
and she said, "Can I watch it?"
and I said, "No."

00:01:38.931 --> 00:01:41.767
It was "Cat on a Hot Tin Roof,"
which starts off kind of --

00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:43.636
-Yeah, very fun.
-Pretty fun

00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:45.638
and then quickly deteriorates
into something else.

00:01:45.638 --> 00:01:47.206
And she said, "Can I come?"
and I said, "You can watch

00:01:47.206 --> 00:01:49.342
the first 10 minutes,"
and she was in the sound box.

00:01:49.342 --> 00:01:50.543
And then I was like, you know,

00:01:50.543 --> 00:01:51.844
then my assistant was going to
take her out.

00:01:51.844 --> 00:01:53.947
She refused to leave,
and it was a live show.

00:01:53.947 --> 00:01:55.782
And she was like, "No, no."

00:01:55.782 --> 00:01:57.417
So she's --
Yeah, she stayed and watched.

00:01:57.417 --> 00:01:59.285
-She out-thought you
on that one.

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:02.121
-She's outwitting me on every
single -- She's like me.

00:02:02.121 --> 00:02:04.623
-It's very smart to be like,
"Yes, I will leave 10 minutes

00:02:04.623 --> 00:02:06.993
into your play" and then be
like, "I'm not going."

00:02:06.993 --> 00:02:09.328
"And I'm going to make
a fair amount of noise."

00:02:09.328 --> 00:02:11.597
-Exactly. And there's nothing
you can do about it.

00:02:11.597 --> 00:02:13.265
Then the following day,
she was very respectful

00:02:13.265 --> 00:02:15.501
and let me sleep in, which
doesn't often happen

00:02:15.501 --> 00:02:16.435
because she's like,
"Mum's an actor."

00:02:16.435 --> 00:02:17.503
That lasted an hour.

00:02:17.503 --> 00:02:18.704
-Oh, that's very sweet. Yeah.

00:02:18.704 --> 00:02:20.039
-One day. One day of respect.

00:02:20.039 --> 00:02:21.607
-Even if you get an hour,
that's something.

00:02:21.607 --> 00:02:22.941
-I know.

00:02:22.941 --> 00:02:24.610
-Your daughter, Marlowe,
which is a beautiful name.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:26.879
You said she's almost 10.

00:02:26.879 --> 00:02:28.915
Do you see yourself in her now?

00:02:28.915 --> 00:02:30.249
I mean, that's an age
where you can tell.

00:02:30.249 --> 00:02:31.884
-She's like me 2.0.

00:02:31.884 --> 00:02:35.254
I mean, she's like -- I think
I had all of what she expresses

00:02:35.254 --> 00:02:38.124
contained inside and just didn't
have the courage to let it out.

00:02:38.124 --> 00:02:40.059
-Oh, that must be fun to see.
-She's extra.

00:02:40.059 --> 00:02:41.995
I mean, she is like
fully out there.

00:02:41.995 --> 00:02:43.897
I mean, kids today,
you've got three now.

00:02:43.897 --> 00:02:45.898
-Yes.
-It's intense.

00:02:45.898 --> 00:02:49.168
And girls in New York City,
she's like, "No, no, no."

00:02:49.168 --> 00:02:51.204
You know?
-It is really amazing growing --

00:02:51.204 --> 00:02:52.838
like, raising kids in New York.
-Yeah.

00:02:52.838 --> 00:02:54.574
-Because I grew up
in the suburbs.

00:02:54.574 --> 00:02:56.242
We walked through Washington
Square Park the other day,

00:02:56.242 --> 00:02:59.478
and my 4-year-old
was the weirdest person

00:02:59.478 --> 00:03:00.880
in Washington Square Park.
-That's really impressive.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:02.915
-Like, loudly weird.
-Wow.

00:03:02.915 --> 00:03:05.852
-He was pulling -- He got out of
the stroller and pulled it

00:03:05.852 --> 00:03:08.320
behind him and kept telling
people he was a UPS guy.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:09.722
[ Laughter ]
-I love him.

00:03:09.722 --> 00:03:11.724
-And you can tell, like,
even, like, people

00:03:11.724 --> 00:03:13.826
in Washington Square Park
who are not doing well.

00:03:13.826 --> 00:03:15.627
-Yes, that's an understatement.

00:03:15.627 --> 00:03:17.663
-And they were like, "This guy
has got some work to do."

00:03:17.663 --> 00:03:19.599
-Wow. Wow.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:19.599 --> 00:03:21.233
Yeah, I mean,
when I was growing up,

00:03:21.233 --> 00:03:22.535
I was just saying sorry
the whole time.

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:23.836
It's very English to do that.
-Yes.

00:03:23.836 --> 00:03:25.504
-She does not have that problem.
-Oh, that's nice, though.

00:03:25.504 --> 00:03:27.006
Yeah.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:03:27.006 --> 00:03:28.374
-I'll use that as a transition.

00:03:28.374 --> 00:03:31.177
This show is a very --
So this is a --

00:03:31.177 --> 00:03:32.712
This is a very British show
as well.

00:03:32.712 --> 00:03:34.480
It's based on a scandal.
-Yes.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:36.115
-And I will say,
I think British scandals

00:03:36.115 --> 00:03:38.284
are my favorite of the scandals.
-I mean, we do scandal well.

00:03:38.284 --> 00:03:39.885
Don't we?
-You do scandal very well.

00:03:39.885 --> 00:03:41.854
Because there's a lot of
pretending it isn't a scandal.

00:03:41.854 --> 00:03:44.256
Like, that clip was very good.
-Oh, sweeping it under the rug.

00:03:44.256 --> 00:03:45.425
-Yes.
-Like, "What scandal?

00:03:45.425 --> 00:03:47.527
What are you talking about?"
-"We'll be fine."

00:03:47.527 --> 00:03:49.729
[ Laughter ]
-What, what, what, what?

00:03:49.729 --> 00:03:51.063
No, it quickly deteriorates into

00:03:51.063 --> 00:03:53.665
full-blown, like,
undeniable scandal.

00:03:53.665 --> 00:03:56.068
You know, it's David E. Kelley
and Melissa James Gibson.

00:03:56.068 --> 00:03:57.804
They did "The Undoing"
and "Big Little Lies."

00:03:57.804 --> 00:03:59.973
And it's that format.
And they're so good at it.

00:03:59.973 --> 00:04:01.407
-Yeah.
-I love those.

00:04:01.407 --> 00:04:04.010
-So this is actually tricky
because I love "The Undoing."

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:07.046
But the British guy, Hugh Grant
in that, he was suspicious

00:04:07.046 --> 00:04:08.380
the whole time
because he was British.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:10.116
-Yes. Yes.
-But in this one, everybody's

00:04:10.116 --> 00:04:11.750
British, so you really
don't know who to trust.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:14.187
[ Laughter ]
-So true.

00:04:14.187 --> 00:04:17.089
Never trust the British men.
-Yeah. I mean, he was --

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:18.791
From the very beginning, you're
like, "Come on, Hugh Grant."

00:04:18.791 --> 00:04:20.226
-It's too much hair to trust.
-Yeah.

00:04:20.226 --> 00:04:21.861
-Right, at that age.
-Absolutely.

00:04:21.861 --> 00:04:23.796
I mean, he looks too good
to be trusted.

00:04:23.796 --> 00:04:26.065
-Yes, exactly.

00:04:26.065 --> 00:04:28.200
-This is based on a book.
Had you read the book?

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:29.402
-I did not read the book.
-Okay.

00:04:29.402 --> 00:04:31.337
-I read the book after I'd read
the six scripts,

00:04:31.337 --> 00:04:32.938
which were so compelling.
-Okay.

00:04:32.938 --> 00:04:34.540
-And then, you know, that thing
of an actor

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:35.974
where you have to really
do the research.

00:04:35.974 --> 00:04:37.577
All of that was done for me
in the book.

00:04:37.577 --> 00:04:38.778
-That's very nice. Yes.
-All of the thoughts --

00:04:38.778 --> 00:04:40.446
It was great.
It's a brilliant book.

00:04:40.446 --> 00:04:43.015
-Do you usually read
a thriller like that?

00:04:43.015 --> 00:04:44.383
Is that a kind of book
you turn to?

00:04:44.383 --> 00:04:47.086
-I love them, and I will take
them on a holiday,

00:04:47.086 --> 00:04:49.422
but I always get guilt that I
haven't read the classics,

00:04:49.422 --> 00:04:51.357
so I kind of punish myself
with my reading.

00:04:51.357 --> 00:04:53.926
Like, "Why am I not reading
'Hardy' that I have not read

00:04:53.926 --> 00:04:55.361
instead of this really
compelling, addictive one?"

00:04:55.361 --> 00:04:57.463
-This is very noble.
So you're still --

00:04:57.463 --> 00:04:58.998
-I mean, it takes me like
10 years.

00:04:58.998 --> 00:05:00.099
I've just finished
"War and Peace" like eight --

00:05:00.099 --> 00:05:01.600
-You did "War and Peace"?
-I did it.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:03.469
-Alright. So this is fascinating
to me because I --

00:05:03.469 --> 00:05:04.937
So, I read "War and Peace."
-Don't ask me what happened.

00:05:04.937 --> 00:05:06.972
-Okay, I won't.
-I understood three words.

00:05:06.972 --> 00:05:09.842
But, yes, I read it.
-But that's what I was gonna
say. I read it in college,

00:05:09.842 --> 00:05:11.511
but it was a -- The whole course
was "War and Peace."

00:05:11.511 --> 00:05:13.246
So we had a professor
that sort of held our hand

00:05:13.246 --> 00:05:15.748
through all of it, and I would
say, like, it was a great --

00:05:15.748 --> 00:05:17.516
like, having someone walk you
through it,

00:05:17.516 --> 00:05:18.584
I'm like,
"This is really great."

00:05:18.584 --> 00:05:20.053
But I would also say, one,

00:05:20.053 --> 00:05:21.320
I don't remember anything
that happened.

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:24.690
And I can't imagine doing it
alone on your own.

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:26.192
So that's very impressive.
-You know, it was good.

00:05:26.192 --> 00:05:28.127
It started in lockdown.
It ended recently.

00:05:28.127 --> 00:05:29.829
It's a long book.
-It's a very long book.

00:05:29.829 --> 00:05:31.831
-But my God, that Tolstoy can
write a book.

00:05:31.831 --> 00:05:33.833
-Oh, you know what?
If you take anything away

00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:36.602
from this interview,
"that Tolstoy can write a book."

00:05:36.602 --> 00:05:39.272
-Yeah, he's good with words.
-He's not too shabby.

00:05:39.272 --> 00:05:40.606
[ Laughter ]

00:05:40.606 --> 00:05:42.942
It's a shame. What happened to
him? Is he still working?

00:05:42.942 --> 00:05:44.777
-I don't think so.
-I don't think so.

00:05:44.777 --> 00:05:46.746
That's Hollywood for you.
They just -- They find

00:05:46.746 --> 00:05:48.447
the hot new thing
and they just move on.

00:05:48.447 --> 00:05:50.015
[ Laughter ]

00:05:50.015 --> 00:05:52.852
And you got to -- This was a
lockdown show as well, so you --

00:05:52.852 --> 00:05:55.888
-Yes. I got to leave
the lockdown to go to work,

00:05:55.888 --> 00:05:58.658
which was really, really lucky.
-Yeah.

00:05:58.658 --> 00:06:00.826
And you got to be in London as
well, or I guess in England.

00:06:00.826 --> 00:06:02.862
Did you shoot in London?
-We shot at Shepperton Studios,

00:06:02.862 --> 00:06:04.864
which is outside of London
and around London.

00:06:04.864 --> 00:06:06.198
-Okay, great.
-A bit of Manchester.

00:06:06.198 --> 00:06:07.366
It was great.

00:06:07.366 --> 00:06:09.669
I was so ready to be around
family and home.

00:06:09.669 --> 00:06:13.238
And I'd done the first bit
in New York, which was intense.

00:06:13.238 --> 00:06:15.274
So I was just ready to go back.

00:06:15.274 --> 00:06:17.977
So we had this year.
-And so were you in actual

00:06:17.977 --> 00:06:20.679
sort of courthouses
or were they courthouse sets?

00:06:20.679 --> 00:06:22.014
-They were sets
because of COVID.

00:06:22.014 --> 00:06:24.750
They had to rebuild the
Old Bailey and places like that,

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:25.918
which they did perfectly.

00:06:25.918 --> 00:06:27.219
Like, we have this incredible
house.

00:06:27.219 --> 00:06:28.387
It was all a set.

00:06:28.387 --> 00:06:30.489
I wanted to just, like, forklift
it somewhere else

00:06:30.489 --> 00:06:32.859
and live in it forever
because it was so nice.

00:06:32.859 --> 00:06:35.194
-I mean, the Old Bailey always,
you know, it seems so historic.

00:06:35.194 --> 00:06:37.463
Was that something when you grew
up in London,

00:06:37.463 --> 00:06:39.298
did you, like, go there
on field trips?

00:06:39.298 --> 00:06:40.532
Is that like a thing you do
as a young --

00:06:40.532 --> 00:06:42.167
-Nope. No. No.
-No?

00:06:42.167 --> 00:06:43.703
-We'd go to like the farm or --

00:06:43.703 --> 00:06:45.137
[ Laughter ]
You know, I wish we did.

00:06:45.137 --> 00:06:47.473
You can actually go and sit in
on real trials,

00:06:47.473 --> 00:06:48.807
which I've always --
I've meant to do,

00:06:48.807 --> 00:06:50.342
but it's one of those things
that I've never done.

00:06:50.342 --> 00:06:51.877
-Well, you're too busy
reading Tolstoy.

00:06:51.877 --> 00:06:53.245
-Exactly, Seth. Yes.

00:06:53.245 --> 00:06:54.313
-You've got a lot of big things
going on.

00:06:54.313 --> 00:06:56.815
-Yes, I'm a busy lady.
Yes, exactly.

00:06:56.815 --> 00:06:58.884
-Well, it is always --
Congratulations on the show.

00:06:58.884 --> 00:07:00.018
-Thank you.
-It's wonderful.

00:07:00.018 --> 00:07:01.120
And it's always so wonderful
to see you.

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:02.120
Thanks so much for being here,
Sienna.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:03.188
-Thanks for having me.

00:07:03.188 --> 00:07:04.791
-Sienna Miller, everybody.

00:07:04.791 --> 00:07:07.693
"Anatomy of a Scandal" begins
streaming April 15th on Netflix.

